                "text": "An analysis of the examples reveals the following transformation rule:\n\n1. **Identify the objects**: The grid contains lines (vertical and/or horizontal) of different colors.\n2. **Find the key feature/change**:\n   - In Example 1: The horizontal line of 5s at row 12 had an original extent from col 1 to col 10. In the output, it extends one step to the left, reaching col 0. Why 5?\n     - 9: length 9 (cols 7 to 15, row 7)\n     - 7: length 5 (rows 8 to 12, col 18)\n     - 3: length 6 (rows 11 to 16, col 14)\n     - 5: length 10 (cols 1 to 10, row 12)\n     Notice lengths: 9 has length 9; 7 has length 5; 3 has length 6; 5 has length 10. Wait, length of 5 is 10. No wait, is 5 the longest? 5 has length 10, 9 has length 9. So 5 is the longest!\n   - In Example 2:\n     - 2: vertical, rows 2 to 10 (length 9). Output: a horizontal branch of 2s is drawn from row 6, cols 0 to 4!\n       Wait! Row 6 is where the 4 begins!\n       Rows of 4: rows 6 to 11 (length 6).\n       Rows of 8: rows 2 to 9 (length 8).\n       Rows of 2: rows 2 to 10 (length 9).\n       Wait, why did row 6 get 2s from col 0 to 4?\n   - In Example 3:\n     - 8: rows 1 to 3 (length 3, col 7)\n     - 6: row 7, cols 1 to 7 (length 7)\n     Output: 6 extends to col 0! (col 0, row 7 is filled with 6).\n\nWait, look at what changed in each example:\n- Example 1: cell (12, 0) becomes 5.\n- Example 2: cells (6, 0), (6, 1), (6, 2), (6, 3) become 2!\n- Example 3: cell (7, 0) becomes 6.\n\nIn all three examples, something extends all the way to the left border (column 0)!\nWait, which row connects to column 0?\n- Example 1: row 12. Row 12 is the row of the 5s! The line of 5s already touched (12, 1) and reached to (12, 10). It just extended left to col 0.\n- Example 2: row 6! Why row 6?\n  What is special about row 6?\n  In row 6, there is 2 (at col 4), 4 (at col 8), 8 (at col 11).\n  Wait, what are the top and bottom of each line?\n  2: [2..10] -> center is (2+10)/2 = 6!\n  Wait! Row 6 is the exact midpoint of the line of 2s!\n  2 runs from row 2 to row 10: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10. Midpoint is 6!\n  And in row 6, it draws to the left, from col 4 to col 0!\n  Wait, why didn't it happen for 8 or 4?\n  Why 2? Because 2 is the longest? Or 2 is leftmost?\n  Let's check 2:\n  Length of 2 = 9.\n  Length of 8 = 8.\n  Length of 4 = 6.\n  2 is the longest vertical line!\n  Wait, what about Example 1?\n  Lines in Example 1:\n  - 5: horizontal at row 12, cols 1..10 (length 10)\n  - 9: horizontal at row 7, cols 7..15 (length 9)\n  - 3: vertical at col 14, rows 11..16 (length 6)\n  - 7: vertical at col 18, rows 8..12 (length 5)\n  Longest line overall is 5 (length 10).\n  Does 5 extend to col 0? Yes! 5 is horizontal, it just extends along its row to col 0!\n  Wait, Example 3:\n  - 8: vertical at col 7, rows 1..3 (length 3)\n  - 6: horizontal at row 7, cols 1..7 (length 7)\n  Longest line is 6 (length 7). It extends horizontally to col 0!\n\nWait! Does the longest line extend to col 0?\nWait, if it's horizontal, it extends along its row to col 0.\nIf it's vertical, does it branch from its midpoint to col 0?\nLet's check if 2's midpoint is row 6:\nStart = 2, End = 10. Midpoint = (2 + 10) / 2 = 6.\nYes! (10 - 2 = 8, 8/2 = 4, 2 + 4 = 6).\nAnd from (6, 4) it extends leftwards to (6, 0)!\n\nI am ready to receive the first test input. Please provide it.",
